        </div>
        <div class="trust-result">
          <span class="trust-rnum">3,400%</span>
          <span class="trust-label">growth in new users for a brand-new web app</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trust-result">
          <span class="trust-rnum">{'<'}30 days</span>
          <span class="trust-label">to get a client cited on ChatGPT (AI search)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 03 · THE PROBLEM (the marketing time trap) ============
       Stats are third-party research, cited in the footnote. Tier-1 sources:
       Constant Contact Small Business Now 2024; Adobe Express / Talker Research
       2026; Harvard Business Review. Do not mix Constant Contact editions. -->
  <section class="section problem" aria-labelledby="gap-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="problem-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The time trap</p>
        <h2 id="gap-title">You’re not bad at marketing. You’re out of time.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          More than half of small business owners get an hour or less a day for marketing. So it happens
          in scraps — a burst of posts, a freelancer, a half-built funnel — then it stalls. That’s not a
          discipline problem. It’s a time problem.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- hero stat -->
      <div class="stat-hero">
        <div class="stat-hero-num" role="img" aria-label="56 percent of small businesses">
          <span class="big" data-countup="56" data-suffix="%" aria-hidden="true">56%</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-hero-body">
          <p>of small businesses have <strong>an hour or less a day</strong> to spend on marketing — and it’s the first thing to slip when the week fills up.</p>
          <div class="stat-bar">
            <span class="stat-bar-fill" data-width="56" style="width:56%"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- three agitations, each stat-led -->
      <div class="problem-grid">
        <div class="problem-item">
          <span class="problem-stat" aria-hidden="true"><span data-countup="73" data-suffix="%">73%</span></span>
          <h3>You can’t tell if it’s working</h3>
          <p>Nearly three-quarters of owners aren’t confident their marketing is paying off. So you keep spending on guesses — and quietly brace for the bill.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-item">
          <span class="problem-stat" aria-hidden="true"><span data-countup="5">5</span> jobs</span>
          <h3>You’re wearing five hats</h3>
          <p>Customer service, sales, the books, social, the actual product. Marketing is the plate that drops first when the day runs out.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-item">
          <span class="problem-stat" aria-hidden="true"><span data-countup="17" data-suffix="%">17%</span></span>
          <h3>Competitors don’t wait</h3>
          <p>Firms that keep marketing through a downturn come out up to 17% stronger. Every month you’re stalled is ground someone else takes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="problem-foot">
        Half-done marketing doesn’t stay still. It bleeds leads, referrals, and revenue to whoever shows up instead.
        <span class="problem-src">Sources: Constant Contact, <em>Small Business Now</em> (2024); Adobe Express / Talker Research (2026); Harvard Business Review.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 04 · HOW IT WORKS (the plan — effort objection) ============ -->
